      ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $-16$ is negative, so it does *not* belong to $N,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-16\\notin N\n$$\n\n- However, $-16$ is an integer, so it does belong to $Z,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n-16\\in Z\n$$\n\n- Every integer is a rational, real, and complex number. Consequently, $-16$ belongs to $Q,R,$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}-16\\in Q\\qquad -16\\in R \\\\ -16\\in C\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $Z, Q, R, C$ only."
